WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.960
Welcome to the Friday show, I have a few stories&nbsp;
I want to run through before we move on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.000
other things, including your questions.
We talked last week about the continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.880
possibility that Donald Trump will start his&nbsp;
own political party. We also saw a poll showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.080
that nearly two thirds of Republican voters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.960
are, in theory, willing to leave the Republican&nbsp;
Party and follow Trump to a new party. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.680
of course, if Donald Trump started a new party&nbsp;
and forget about 64 percent, if Donald Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.400
Trump took 50 percent or 40 percent or even 30&nbsp;
percent of Republican voters to a new party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.360
it could spell absolute disaster for the American&nbsp;
right, splitting the vote and allowing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:45.440
Democratic Party to win a lot to take a phrase out&nbsp;
of Donald Trump's vocabulary. There's now another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:51.200
new story, and this is being reported by Reuters.&nbsp;
There are dozens of former Republican officials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:57.156
who view the Republican Party as unwilling to&nbsp;
stand up to Donald Trump. So this is an anti&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.156 --> 00:01:02.720
Trump group of former Republican officials.&nbsp;
And what these individuals are saying is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:08.400
if the Republican Party won't stand up to Trump&nbsp;
and say, no, we reject Trump ism and we reject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:15.120
Trump, they are in discussions to break away from&nbsp;
the Republican Party and start their own. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.960
this includes former officials in the Republican&nbsp;
administrations, Ronald Reagan, George H.W. Bush,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:28.560
George W. Bush, as well as even disaffected&nbsp;
Trump aides and staffers and political employees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.600
This includes some Republican ambassadors. This&nbsp;
includes some Republican strategists and others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:40.160
And 120 of them had a zoom call, of course,&nbsp;
last Friday to have a conversation about this.&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.480
And the idea is they would say the Republican&nbsp;
Party has become the party of Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.560
We are getting back to principled conservatism,&nbsp;
as they call it. What that means is pretending to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.800
care about the debt and deficit. Let's not mistake&nbsp;
that for anything else. But it doesn't matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:01.760
because, again, this could also be an absolute and&nbsp;
total disaster for the Republican Party. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:09.920
the people involved is Evan McMullen. You might&nbsp;
remember he ran third party in 2016, former CIA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:16.800
agent, I believe, and he is part of this movement&nbsp;
for a possible splinter party. I have to tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:23.520
that this is fantastic news. Imagine if we&nbsp;
got. But these would be two gifts if Donald,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.320
you've got the Republican Party and within it&nbsp;
you have people who want to stick with Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.360
and people who want the Republican Party to&nbsp;
completely denounce and distance from Donald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:39.600
Trump. You could have a situation where so-called&nbsp;
principled conservatives in the Republican Party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:45.280
split off and start their own party to get away&nbsp;
from Trump. And at the same time, Donald Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:53.280
starts his own party and takes the hyper pro Trump&nbsp;
people away as well. So you could end up with. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.680
Trump Party taking away from the Republican Party,&nbsp;
the principled conservative former Republicans who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.240
are anti Trump splitting off from the Republican&nbsp;
Party, and then I guess what you'd have left in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.560
the Republican Party are just generally people&nbsp;
not paying much attention or who don't don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.480
really feel strongly one way or the other. And&nbsp;
they just want to keep voting for people like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.000
I don't know, Lindsey Graham. And then they&nbsp;
stay. And you could have a Republican Party split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.800
three ways. It I have no interest in giving&nbsp;
advice to these folks about what they should do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:29.120
I simply recognize the mathematical realities that&nbsp;
even if Trump splits off and takes 15 percent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:36.000
the party and these principled conservatives split&nbsp;
off and take 10 or 15 percent of the party, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:43.280
creates a situation in which Republicans, as&nbsp;
we know them or in any of these new iterations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:49.760
don't win for a decade. And if there would ever&nbsp;
be an opportunity for the left to make progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.400
in the United States, it would be that now,&nbsp;
unfortunately, the Democratic Party has shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:01.680
itself so often to be so incompetent that it&nbsp;
would probably still end up being a challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:07.600
for a Democratic Party. Given the gift of a&nbsp;
Republican Party divided three ways, it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.240
probably still end up being an uphill battle for&nbsp;
the modern Democratic Party to make progress. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.160
at least they'd have the opportunity. We'll&nbsp;
follow it. Very, very interesting stuff.

